WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.818
Welcome to Module 2 of the
Provider Notices learning path:

2
00:00:03.818 --> 00:00:05.938
Azure Service Health and alerts.

3
00:00:05.938 --> 00:00:09.358
This is Gate 1 of the provider
notices implementation guide,

4
00:00:09.358 --> 00:00:12.163
with the first chapter of
the developer workbook.

5
00:00:12.163 --> 00:00:17.108
Service Health is where Azure publishes
service issues, planned maintenance, health

6
00:00:17.108 --> 00:00:21.775
advisories, retirements among them, and
security advisories for a subscription.

7
00:00:21.775 --> 00:00:25.912
In this module you read the fixture
subscriptions with the kit's query,

8
00:00:25.912 --> 00:00:28.337
built from Microsoft Learn's own samples.

9
00:00:28.337 --> 00:00:32.843
Then you plan the built-in Azure Policy
that creates Service Health alerts in

10
00:00:32.843 --> 00:00:37.700
every subscription, as a plan that changes
nothing until someone approves its hash.

11
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:39.963
It takes about fifty-five minutes,

12
00:00:39.963 --> 00:00:43.600
with a lab on fixtures and a
lab for a test subscription.

13
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:49.450
The code is illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not run against a live tenant.

14
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.150
Why does Azure need a gate of its own?

15
00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:58.475
Service Health shows service
issues, planned maintenance,

16
00:00:58.475 --> 00:01:02.275
health advisories and security
advisories for each subscription.

17
00:01:02.275 --> 00:01:06.734
But the portal tells nobody unless
someone created a Service Health alert,

18
00:01:06.734 --> 00:01:11.625
and an alert fires only for the subscriptions,
services and regions its rule names.

19
00:01:11.625 --> 00:01:14.977
Microsoft's own F A Q gives
a rule that left out the

20
00:01:14.977 --> 00:01:19.000
affected subscription as a
reason an alert never arrived.

21
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.117
Azure Resource Graph answers the same
questions for every subscription in scope with

22
00:01:24.117 --> 00:01:27.315
one query, against its Service
Health resources table,

23
00:01:27.315 --> 00:01:30.300
and Microsoft Learn
publishes the sample queries.

24
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.375
So this module does two things.

25
00:01:32.375 --> 00:01:36.013
It collects with the kit's query,
built from Learn's samples.

26
00:01:36.013 --> 00:01:39.844
And it plans Service Health alerts
at scale with Azure Policy,

27
00:01:39.844 --> 00:01:43.270
as a plan that's applied
only with an approved hash.

28
00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:49.933
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

29
00:01:49.933 --> 00:01:53.318
You'll name the least-privileged
roles that read Service Health,

30
00:01:53.318 --> 00:01:56.257
and the one a security advisory's
sensitive fields need.

31
00:01:56.257 --> 00:01:59.494
You'll compare the kit's events
query with Learn's samples.

32
00:01:59.494 --> 00:02:04.545
You'll collect the fixture subscriptions
and read each event's kind and action date.

33
00:02:04.545 --> 00:02:08.125
And you'll plan Service Health
alerts at scale with Azure Policy,

34
00:02:08.125 --> 00:02:10.895
and show that fixture mode
never applies the plan.

35
00:02:10.895 --> 00:02:14.570
You need Module 1, for the
record and its action basis.

36
00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:19.994
If you have time, read the Azure platform
and Microsoft 365 audit telemetry chapter

37
00:02:19.994 --> 00:02:22.510
of Security Operations and Telemetry,

38
00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:27.620
which sends the Azure activity log's
Service Health category to the workspace.

39
00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:31.782
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1
or later with Pester 5.

40
00:02:31.782 --> 00:02:33.060
No tenant.

41
00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:40.022
Unit 1 is the guide's
Step 1, in four parts.

42
00:02:40.022 --> 00:02:43.923
First, grant the reader its roles:
Reader on each subscription

43
00:02:43.923 --> 00:02:47.135
in scope is enough to see
its Service Health events.

44
00:02:47.135 --> 00:02:49.870
But a security advisory's
sensitive fields,

45
00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:53.973
and its impacted resources, need
Service Health Security Reader.

46
00:02:53.973 --> 00:02:56.410
Reader and Monitoring
Reader can't see them.

47
00:02:56.410 --> 00:03:00.972
Second, sign in in the right cloud,
with the Resource Graph extension.

48
00:03:00.972 --> 00:03:04.358
A GCC agency's subscriptions
are commercial Azure,

49
00:03:04.358 --> 00:03:07.985
the Azure Cloud setting;
Azure Government uses its own.

50
00:03:07.985 --> 00:03:13.397
Third, collect, with the kit's events
query and Learn's impacted-resources query.

51
00:03:13.397 --> 00:03:16.688
And fourth, alert at scale:
plan the built-in policy

52
00:03:16.688 --> 00:03:19.898
at the management group,
with a remediation task.

53
00:03:19.898 --> 00:03:23.224
The gate passes when every
subscription's events are

54
00:03:23.224 --> 00:03:26.398
listed and each security
advisory has its summary.

55
00:03:26.398 --> 00:03:30.770
An empty summary is the sign that
the reader lacks the elevated role.

56
00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:36.482
Unit 2 reads the workbook chapter,

57
00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:40.195
starting with what Microsoft gives
you and what it leaves to you.

58
00:03:40.195 --> 00:03:45.407
Learn publishes one sample query per question:
all active events, health advisories,

59
00:03:45.407 --> 00:03:50.545
upcoming retirements and planned maintenance,
each returning different columns.

60
00:03:50.545 --> 00:03:54.514
The kit builds one events query from
the all-active sample, adding the

61
00:03:54.514 --> 00:03:58.970
retirement sample's event subtype and
the advisory sample's time conversion.

62
00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:02.195
Learn's samples return one row
per event per subscription,

63
00:04:02.195 --> 00:04:05.420
so the same outage would be
counted once per subscription.

64
00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:09.270
The kit merges rows by
tracking I D into one notice.

65
00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:12.386
Learn gives event types,
not what to do about them,

66
00:04:12.386 --> 00:04:14.645
so the kit maps them to the six kinds.

67
00:04:14.645 --> 00:04:19.620
Nothing says action is required, so the
kit states a rule, and the record names it.

68
00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:22.857
And for the alert policy,
Learn gives portal steps.

69
00:04:22.857 --> 00:04:27.040
The kit writes a plan file a
reviewer can approve before it runs.

70
00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:33.669
The Azure collector
makes two declared calls:

71
00:04:33.669 --> 00:04:36.852
the events query and the
impacted-resources query.

72
00:04:36.852 --> 00:04:41.902
Each is an Azure Resource Graph query in
live mode, and a file in fixture mode.

73
00:04:41.902 --> 00:04:44.702
Then it maps each event type to a kind.

74
00:04:44.702 --> 00:04:46.566
Service issues are incidents.

75
00:04:46.566 --> 00:04:48.715
Planned maintenance is maintenance.

76
00:04:48.715 --> 00:04:53.101
A health advisory whose event subtype
is Retirement is a retirement,

77
00:04:53.101 --> 00:04:55.641
and it's action required
by the kit's rule,

78
00:04:55.641 --> 00:05:00.027
due at the advisory's end time, which
Learn says is its mitigation time.

79
00:05:00.027 --> 00:05:02.728
Any other health advisory is an advisory.

80
00:05:02.728 --> 00:05:07.056
A security advisory is action
required by the kit's rule,

81
00:05:07.056 --> 00:05:09.691
with no date, for the ISSO to review.

82
00:05:09.691 --> 00:05:13.677
And anything else, such as
billing, is left out and counted.

83
00:05:13.677 --> 00:05:18.528
Resources come from the impacted-resources
rows with the same tracking I D.

84
00:05:18.528 --> 00:05:22.991
The source link is the event on the
cloud's own Resource Manager host.

85
00:05:22.991 --> 00:05:26.790
An unknown cloud name
throws; there is no default.

86
00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:33.440
Between collections,
alerts carry the news.

87
00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:37.635
Learn's page on deploying Service
Health alerts at scale names a built-in

88
00:05:37.635 --> 00:05:42.740
definition: configure subscriptions to enable
Service Health monitoring alert rules.

89
00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:47.435
It's assigned at a management group or
subscription, never a resource group,

90
00:05:47.435 --> 00:05:50.692
with a remediation task
so existing subscriptions

91
00:05:50.692 --> 00:05:53.115
get the alert rule too, not only new ones.

92
00:05:53.115 --> 00:05:57.002
With no parameters, the remediation
creates a resource group,

93
00:05:57.002 --> 00:06:00.508
the alert rule and an action
group in each subscription,

94
00:06:00.508 --> 00:06:04.090
and emails the subscription
owners for every event type.

95
00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:08.594
The kit's alert policy script reads the
definition, checks its display name,

96
00:06:08.594 --> 00:06:11.428
and writes a plan with
two steps: the assignment,

97
00:06:11.428 --> 00:06:15.714
with a system-assigned managed identity
granted the definition's role,

98
00:06:15.714 --> 00:06:19.928
because only the portal grants it
automatically, and the remediation.

99
00:06:19.928 --> 00:06:23.629
It prints the plan's
SHA-256 and changes nothing

100
00:06:23.629 --> 00:06:27.141
unless it's run again
with apply and that hash.

101
00:06:27.141 --> 00:06:31.160
And never edit the alert rules
by hand: change the assignment's

102
00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:36.020
parameters and remediate again, or
the policy may overwrite the edit.

103
00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:40.495
Try it.

104
00:06:40.495 --> 00:06:44.376
Lab 1 runs in the provider
notices folder of the examples,

105
00:06:44.376 --> 00:06:47.845
in PowerShell 7, against
fictional subscriptions.

106
00:06:47.845 --> 00:06:49.633
Nothing signs in to anything.

107
00:06:49.633 --> 00:06:51.795
Step one lists what Azure needs.

108
00:06:51.795 --> 00:06:55.850
Two calls, the Service Health
events and the impacted resources,

109
00:06:55.850 --> 00:06:59.445
and each needs only Reader on
each subscription in scope.

110
00:06:59.445 --> 00:07:03.686
The elevated role is Service Health
Security Reader, to see the sensitive

111
00:07:03.686 --> 00:07:07.857
fields of security advisories, which
Reader and Monitoring Reader can't.

112
00:07:07.857 --> 00:07:09.444
Both are marked verified.

113
00:07:09.444 --> 00:07:11.332
Notice that nothing here writes.

114
00:07:11.332 --> 00:07:13.846
Step two prints the kit's events query.

115
00:07:13.846 --> 00:07:16.675
It starts the way Learn's
all-active sample does,

116
00:07:16.675 --> 00:07:21.320
from the Service Health resources table,
filtered to Resource Health events.

117
00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:24.286
It adds the retirement
sample's event subtype,

118
00:07:24.286 --> 00:07:28.345
converts the impact times the
way Learn's advisory sample does,

119
00:07:28.345 --> 00:07:32.840
and ends ordered by tracking I D,
so the rows page in a stable order.

120
00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:42.665
Step three collects Azure from the day-one
fixtures, as of November ninth, 2026.

121
00:07:42.665 --> 00:07:47.499
Five notices, all open, two needing
action: one each of advisory,

122
00:07:47.499 --> 00:07:51.290
incident, maintenance,
retirement and security.

123
00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:56.487
Five notices from six rows, because the
incident appears in two subscriptions,

124
00:07:56.487 --> 00:07:58.965
and the kit merges rows by tracking I D.

125
00:07:58.965 --> 00:08:02.728
Without that merge, the same
outage would become two tasks.

126
00:08:02.728 --> 00:08:04.728
Step four reads the records.

127
00:08:04.728 --> 00:08:09.228
The incident is a virtual machine
connectivity issue in East U S.

128
00:08:09.228 --> 00:08:15.078
The advisory asks clients of Azure
Storage to move to T L S 1.2 or later.

129
00:08:15.078 --> 00:08:19.216
The maintenance is planned work on
virtual machines in East U S 2.

130
00:08:19.216 --> 00:08:23.221
The retirement, of Basic S
K U public I P addresses,

131
00:08:23.221 --> 00:08:27.828
is due on September thirtieth,
2027, at the advisory's end.

132
00:08:27.828 --> 00:08:32.845
And the security advisory, for
Azure Kubernetes Service clusters,

133
00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:36.770
needs action with no date,
for the ISSO to review.

134
00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:42.817
Step five plans the Service Health alert

135
00:08:42.817 --> 00:08:45.520
policy for a fictional
production environment.

136
00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:47.470
Two steps, both would apply.

137
00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:51.815
A L oh one creates the policy assignment
for the built-in definition at the

138
00:08:51.815 --> 00:08:56.658
management group, with a system-assigned
identity, its role and its identity scope.

139
00:08:56.658 --> 00:08:59.183
A L oh two creates the remediation.

140
00:08:59.183 --> 00:09:05.707
Then the plan's SHA-256, and the line
that matters: dry run, nothing changed.

141
00:09:05.707 --> 00:09:10.671
To apply, add apply and the approved
hash, after the change record approves it.

142
00:09:10.671 --> 00:09:15.407
The role I D in the fixture is
made up; Lab 2 reads the real one.

143
00:09:15.407 --> 00:09:19.891
Step six tries to apply the plan in
fixture mode, with a hash of zeros,

144
00:09:19.891 --> 00:09:25.271
and the script refuses before it even
reads the hash: fixture mode never applies.

145
00:09:25.271 --> 00:09:30.057
Step seven runs this chapter's
tests: twenty-six pass, none fail.

146
00:09:30.057 --> 00:09:32.250
Then delete the output folder.

147
00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:39.659
Lab 2 runs the same gate in
an Azure test subscription,

148
00:09:39.659 --> 00:09:41.987
in a test tenant, never production.

149
00:09:41.987 --> 00:09:45.886
You need a reader identity, a person
who can create policy and role

150
00:09:45.886 --> 00:09:49.712
assignments there, and a second
person to approve the plan's hash.

151
00:09:49.712 --> 00:09:53.109
The subscription may have no
Service Health events at all,

152
00:09:53.109 --> 00:09:54.524
and that's a valid result.

153
00:09:54.524 --> 00:09:58.447
Grant the reader Reader and Service
Health Security Reader, sign in,

154
00:09:58.447 --> 00:10:03.068
and cross-check Learn's own query against
the kit's collection; the counts should

155
00:10:03.068 --> 00:10:07.550
agree, once the merge of one event across
subscriptions is taken into account.

156
00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:10.557
Then plan the alert policy
at the test subscription,

157
00:10:10.557 --> 00:10:14.962
apply it with the approved hash, and
check the remediation and the alert rule.

158
00:10:14.962 --> 00:10:18.649
To back it out, plan the rollback,
have it approved, apply it,

159
00:10:18.649 --> 00:10:22.963
delete what the remediation created,
and remove the reader's roles.

160
00:10:22.963 --> 00:10:26.851
Deleting the assignment doesn't
delete what its remediation made.

161
00:10:26.851 --> 00:10:32.390
The authors haven't run Lab 2; the
workbook's Chapter 1 has the exact commands.

162
00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:37.040
Which cloud?

163
00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:41.865
A GCC Moderate estate's subscriptions
are commercial Azure, so the settings

164
00:10:41.865 --> 00:10:46.690
name the Azure Cloud, and the source
links are on management dot azure dot com.

165
00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:49.677
There's no default: an
unknown cloud name throws.

166
00:10:49.677 --> 00:10:55.418
As a side note for GCC High and DoD estates,
Azure Government uses its own cloud

167
00:10:55.418 --> 00:11:01.428
setting and its own Resource Manager host,
management dot us gov cloud api dot net.

168
00:11:01.428 --> 00:11:06.243
Every Service Health sample on Learn links
the Azure Government portal as well as

169
00:11:06.243 --> 00:11:09.502
the global one, but an explicit
availability statement

170
00:11:09.502 --> 00:11:12.689
for the table in Azure
Government is not yet specified.

171
00:11:12.689 --> 00:11:15.228
The danger is a reader in the wrong cloud.

172
00:11:15.228 --> 00:11:19.729
Azure Government subscriptions read from
the commercial cloud return nothing,

173
00:11:19.729 --> 00:11:21.878
and the gate would pass on an empty list.

174
00:11:21.878 --> 00:11:26.840
So the live invoker refuses an Azure
command line set to another cloud.

175
00:11:30.590 --> 00:11:31.977
The federal requirement.

176
00:11:31.977 --> 00:11:38.241
Gate 1 supports NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three S A nine, part c,

177
00:11:38.241 --> 00:11:42.661
monitoring the provider on an
ongoing basis; S I five, part a,

178
00:11:42.661 --> 00:11:48.740
receiving security alerts and advisories;
and C A seven, continuous monitoring.

179
00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:51.657
The evidence starts with
the permission list: Reader,

180
00:11:51.657 --> 00:11:56.365
and Service Health Security Reader for
security advisories, and nothing that writes.

181
00:11:56.365 --> 00:11:58.320
Then the raw Azure response,

182
00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:02.315
the notices and the manifest,
with every call and its role.

183
00:12:02.315 --> 00:12:06.109
For the alert path that runs
between collections, the alert plan,

184
00:12:06.109 --> 00:12:10.190
the results of the apply, and the
change record that approved the hash.

185
00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:13.616
And in Lab 2, the change
record for the rollback,

186
00:12:13.616 --> 00:12:16.140
which supports C M three and C P two.

187
00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:18.952
The gate's output is
evidence for these controls;

188
00:12:18.952 --> 00:12:23.240
your organization still sets their
parameters, and the assessor decides.

189
00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:27.277
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers,

190
00:12:27.277 --> 00:12:31.610
and which file supports which
indicator is the program's mapping.

191
00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:37.335
Check yourself with two questions.

192
00:12:37.335 --> 00:12:41.610
First: a security advisory's
summary is empty in the record.

193
00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:43.160
What's the likely cause?

194
00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:44.385
The answer is b.

195
00:12:44.385 --> 00:12:47.963
The reader holds Reader but not
Service Health Security Reader,

196
00:12:47.963 --> 00:12:49.785
which the sensitive fields need.

197
00:12:49.785 --> 00:12:53.095
Reader and Monitoring Reader
can list the advisory,

198
00:12:53.095 --> 00:12:56.097
but can't see its summary
or impacted resources.

199
00:12:56.097 --> 00:12:59.798
Second: the same incident
appears in two subscriptions.

200
00:12:59.798 --> 00:13:01.898
How many notices does the kit write?

201
00:13:01.898 --> 00:13:03.734
The answer is b, one.

202
00:13:03.734 --> 00:13:07.725
Rows are merged by tracking I
D, so one event is one notice,

203
00:13:07.725 --> 00:13:10.847
and later one task, not
one per subscription.

204
00:13:10.847 --> 00:13:13.275
The module page has three more questions,

205
00:13:13.275 --> 00:13:16.120
each linked to the
section that answers it.

206
00:13:19.870 --> 00:13:20.695
To sum up.

207
00:13:20.695 --> 00:13:23.262
You listed the two roles
Azure's reader needs,

208
00:13:23.262 --> 00:13:25.760
read the kit's query
against Learn's samples,

209
00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:30.618
collected five notices from the fixture
subscriptions, and planned Service Health

210
00:13:30.618 --> 00:13:34.920
alerts at the management group, as a
plan that fixture mode refuses to apply.

211
00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:39.313
The evidence an ISSO keeps for
Gate 1 is the raw Azure response,

212
00:13:39.313 --> 00:13:44.567
the notices as JSON and as a spreadsheet
file, and the manifest, all dated;

213
00:13:44.567 --> 00:13:49.391
the permission list from step one;
and the alert plan with its SHA-256,

214
00:13:49.391 --> 00:13:54.645
and in Lab 2, the change record that
approved it and the results of the apply.

215
00:13:54.645 --> 00:13:57.720
Before you ship, work through
the chapter's checklist.

216
00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:02.795
Next is Module 3: the
Microsoft 365 Message center.

217
00:14:02.795 --> 00:14:07.574
That's Gate 2, with the workbook's
second chapter: two Graph permissions,

218
00:14:07.574 --> 00:14:10.940
and why a tenant must be
read on its own Graph host.
